WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.699
A card terminal sends payment messages over TCP: a 4-byte length, a type byte, and a body.

2
00:00:09.699 --> 00:00:13.743
But TCP delivers bytes, cut into chunks of any size.

3
00:00:13.867 --> 00:00:19.734
Netty's decoder keeps every byte so far in one buffer, and runs decode on each chunk.

4
00:00:19.734 --> 00:00:21.598
The first chunk is 3 bytes.

5
00:00:21.598 --> 00:00:22.633
What can it do?

6
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:25.426
Nothing.

7
00:00:25.426 --> 00:00:27.850
3 bytes aren't even the length.

8
00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:29.492
It returns and waits.

9
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.177
The next chunk brings 10.

10
00:00:31.177 --> 00:00:35.150
decode peeks at the length, 5, without moving the reader index.

11
00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:41.268
9 bytes are there, so it skips the length and passes the frame on, as a slice of the same memory.

12
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.977
The rest says 3, but there's no body yet.

13
00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:47.749
So it returns again, and the last chunk completes the frame.

14
00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:50.278
Replies go the other way.

15
00:00:50.278 --> 00:00:51.883
ctx.write never blocks.

16
00:00:51.883 --> 00:00:59.071
Netty queues the bytes in your own memory, and the socket drains them only as fast as the client reads.

17
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.820
This client sends fast and reads slowly.

18
00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.523
What happens to the queue?

19
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.875
It keeps growing: past a megabyte in the lesson's demo.

20
00:01:09.875 --> 00:01:13.974
So Netty marks two levels, 64 KiB high and 32 KiB low.

21
00:01:13.974 --> 00:01:17.389
Past the high mark, isWritable() turns false.

22
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:20.344
Netty still takes your writes, so you act.

23
00:01:20.344 --> 00:01:25.219
setAutoRead(false) stops reading from the client, and TCP slows it down.

24
00:01:25.219 --> 00:01:27.589
Below the low mark, you read again.

25
00:01:28.367 --> 00:01:29.901
Last, the memory itself.

26
00:01:29.901 --> 00:01:34.439
Each ByteBuf comes from a pool of direct memory, and counts its owners.

27
00:01:34.439 --> 00:01:39.106
It starts at 1, release takes one off, and at 0 it goes back to the pool.

28
00:01:39.106 --> 00:01:41.471
The last one to touch it releases it.

29
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:47.964
This audit handler copies the message and passes the copy on, but never releases the original.

30
00:01:47.964 --> 00:01:49.251
What happens to it?

31
00:01:51.433 --> 00:01:54.123
Its count stays at 1, and nothing throws.

32
00:01:54.123 --> 00:01:58.978
Every message leaks one more, until the service runs out of direct memory.

33
00:01:58.978 --> 00:02:01.864
PARANOID leak detection in tests reports it.

34
00:02:02.733 --> 00:02:06.601
The same three rules in code, Java first, then Kotlin.

35
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.157
So frame the bytes before you decode them.

36
00:02:12.157 --> 00:02:14.226
Stop reading when you can't write.

37
00:02:14.226 --> 00:02:17.513
And give every buffer one last owner, who releases it.

38
00:02:17.633 --> 00:02:18.507
Quick check.

39
00:02:18.507 --> 00:02:23.310
A frame's length field says 7, and it arrives as 2, 4 and 6 bytes.

40
00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:26.293
After which chunk does decode pass it on?
